dark-matter.residue-soldered-invariant-n.formal:
Let $(A, V)$ denote a Clifford algebra together with its generating vector subspace $V \subset A$ (the soldering: $V$ is the image of the metric's tangent space, and $v^{2} = g(v,v)$ for $v \in V$). Then:
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(i)] any algebra isomorphism $(A, V) \to (A', V')$ carrying $V$ onto $V'$ preserves the signature $(p,q)$, hence the residue: the residue is an invariant of the soldered algebra;
\item[(ii)] as abstract algebras, residues $0$ and $2$, $3$ and $7$, and $4$ and $6$ are pairwise isomorphic at equal dimension, $\Cl(1,1) \cong \Cl(2,0)$, $\Cl(1,2) \cong \Cl(3,0)$, $\Cl(1,3) \cong \Cl(0,4) \cong \Mat_2(\HH)$; in particular this world's algebra and the $r = 4$ tower's are the same algebra, and no isomorphism between them carries vectors to vectors.
\end{enumerate}
So the tower charge is an invariant of the operator algebra soldered to the metric, not of the algebra: what forbids a portal in the section “Dark, stable, counted” is the one metric, P1.

dark-matter.two-families-are-two-chirali.formal:
In even dimension $2m$:
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(i)] every maximal isotropic $U$ has a pure spinor, unique up to scale, and it is a chirality eigenstate; the pure spinor of $W$ is the null blade $f$ itself, of Fock grade zero, and the pure spinor of $W'$ is the top state $v_{1}\cdots v_{m}f$, of Fock grade $m$;
\item[(ii)] the chirality of $\psi_{U}$ is preserved by even versors (the spin group) and reversed by odd versors (reflections), so the \emph{family} of $U$, defined as the chirality of its pure spinor, is an invariant of the connected isometry group and not of the full one;
\item[(iii)] there are exactly two families, and a Witt pair $W$, $W'$ lies in opposite families if and only if $m$ is odd.
\end{enumerate}
In odd dimension $2m + 1$ there is one family: the connected isometry group acts transitively on maximal isotropic subspaces, and the spin algebra does not preserve the grade parity of $S$.

odd-extensions.doubling-or-complexificat.formal:
Let $n$ be odd and let $\Cl^{0}$ be the even subalgebra of $\Cl(p,q)$.
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(i)] If $\omega^{2} = +1$, then $\Cl(p,q) \cong \Cl^{0} \oplus \Cl^{0}$, the two summands the ideals of the complementary central idempotents $(1 \pm \omega)/2$, and on the summand of $(1 \pm \omega)/2$ the generator $e_{n}$ acts as $\pm s\,\omega'$, with $s = e_{n}^{2}$ and $\omega' = e_{1}\cdots e_{n-1}$ the volume element of the shadow (so the two summands carry opposite signs).
\item[(ii)] If $\omega^{2} = -1$, then $\Cl(p,q) \cong \Cl^{0} \otimes_{\R} \mathrm{span}\{1, \omega\}$, the field $\mathrm{span}\{1, \omega\} \cong \C$ having the central $\omega$ as its unit.
\item[(iii)] $\Cl^{0}(p, q+1) \cong \Cl(p,q)$ and $\Cl^{0}(p+1, q) \cong \Cl(q,p)$.
\end{enumerate}

synthesis.premise-minimal-non-commutativ.formal:
\emph{Premise (F2), minimal non-commutativity.} Grant Premise (F1), the bit, with $n \ge 1$ distinctions. A group $G$, generated by the directions $e_{1}, \dots, e_{n}$ and a bit $c$, extends the configurations $\mathbb{F}_{2}^{n}$ by that one central bit, and distinct directions do not commute:
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(F2a)] $c$ is central and $c^{2} = 1$;
\item[(F2b)] $G/\langle c\rangle$ is elementary abelian of order $2^{n}$ with basis the images of the $e_{i}$, so that $S \mapsto e_{S}\langle c\rangle$, $e_{S}$ the increasing product of the $e_{i}$ with $i \in S$, identifies $\mathbb{F}_{2}^{n}$ under symmetric difference with $G/\langle c\rangle$;
\item[(F2c)] $c \ne 1$ and $e_{i}e_{j} = c\,e_{j}e_{i}$ for all $i \ne j$.
\end{enumerate}
Which directions square to $c$ rather than to $1$ (each $e_{i}^{2}$ lies in $\langle c\rangle$ by (F2b)) is not fixed by the premise: every choice, the signature, is allowed and none is singled out.

\emph{Status and cost.} A chosen primitive of the foundation, mathematical and not physical. It is minimal in two senses: the extension is by the smallest nontrivial group, one bit, and the commutator of any two distinct directions is that bit. The clause $c^{2} = 1$ is forced by (F2b) and (F2c) when $n \ge 2$, and is independent of them when $n = 1$. Once $c^{2} = 1$ holds, centrality is implied as well, since $\langle c\rangle$ is then a normal subgroup of order $2$; so for $n \ge 2$ all of (F2a) follows from (F2b) and (F2c), and (F2a) has content only at $n = 1$, through its order clause (without it, a dihedral group with $c$ a rotation satisfies (F2b) and (F2c) with $c$ not central). What it costs: order now matters, by exactly one bit. What it does not say: anything about addition or coefficients (that is the premise of superposition), or which signature nature uses. No observation bears on it directly; the reading that the sign of a spinor under a full turn is this bit is argued, not proved.

transport.classification-idempotent-orbi.formal:
Let $A = \Cl(p,q)$, nondegenerate, so that $A \cong \Mat_m(D)$ or $\Mat_m(D) \oplus \Mat_m(D)$ with $D \in \{\R, \C, \HH\}$ (Theorem \claimref{transport.classification-real-clifford-algebras}), acting on the free right $D$-module $V = D^m$ (or on the pair of them). Define the \emph{rank} of an idempotent as $\dim_D$ of its image (a rank pair, in the split case). Then:
\begin{enumerate}
\item Two idempotents are conjugate under the inner automorphism group of $A$ if and only if they have the same rank data. In the simple case the idempotents of rank $r$, $0 < r < m$, form a single conjugacy orbit; in the split case inner automorphisms act componentwise and the orbits are indexed by rank pairs.
\item $e$ and $1 - e$ are conjugate if and only if $\mathrm{rank}\, e = m - \mathrm{rank}\, e$ (componentwise, in the split case): the labelling of a complementary pair is gauge exactly when the pair is balanced, and is canonically ordered by rank otherwise.
\item An idempotent is central if and only if its conjugation orbit is a singleton. The taxonomy's P/C boundary is therefore orbit-theoretic: type C comprises the idempotents that conjugation cannot move at all, type P those it moves through a full rank class.
\end{enumerate}
